# bac2food — reproducible environment, as a Singularity/Apptainer image.
#
#   apptainer build bac2food.sif bac2food.def
#
# Build produces a single .sif file. It runs as you, needs no daemon and no root at run
# time, which is why it works on clusters where Docker is not available.
#
# The image carries the CODE and its dependencies. It deliberately does NOT carry the
# reference tables: they are 2.1 GB, they are versioned separately on Zenodo, and one
# source's terms do not permit redistribution inside an image. Mount them at run time.
#
# It builds by CLONING THE PUBLISHED REPOSITORY rather than by copying this working
# directory, and that is deliberate. Apptainer has no .dockerignore: a `%files . /opt`
# would sweep in whatever happens to sit in the working tree — the raw source tables, the
# 248 MB ChEBI ontology, the cohort annotations. Cloning makes the image contain exactly
# what was published and nothing else, and it cannot accidentally include a licensed
# table, because the repository holds none.
#
# To build from a local working copy instead, replace the clone in %post with a %files
# section listing the code directories explicitly — never `. /opt/bac2food`.

Bootstrap: docker
From: python:3.10.8-slim-bookworm

%labels
    Description  Predict which foods feed the bacteria in a metagenome, from their enzymes
    License      AGPL-3.0-or-later (software)

%post
    set -eu

    # procps is for the memory reporting the predictor does at startup. git and the CA
    # bundle are build-time only and are removed again below, so neither is baked in.
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends procps git ca-certificates

    # Pin this to a release tag before you cite the image in a paper:
    #   git clone --depth 1 --branch v1.0.0 ...
    # A tag makes the image reproducible; `main` moves.
    git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/Sanrrone/bac2food.git /opt/bac2food

    cd /opt/bac2food
    pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

    apt-get purge -y git
    apt-get autoremove -y
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* /opt/bac2food/.git

    # Mount point for the reference tables. Apptainer will not create a bind target that
    # does not exist in an immutable image, so it has to be here at build time.
    mkdir -p /data/bac2food

%environment
    export PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
    export BAC2FOOD_CACHE=/data/bac2food/cache

%runscript
    exec python /opt/bac2food/4_predict/bac2food_predict.py "$@"

%help
    Predict which foods feed the bacteria in a metagenome, from their enzymes.

    The reference tables are not in this image. Bind the Zenodo deposit over
    /data/bac2food and give the run a writable place for its derived caches:

      apptainer run --cleanenv \
          --bind /path/to/deposit:/data/bac2food/exports:ro \
          --bind "$PWD/work":/data/bac2food/cache \
          bac2food.sif --mag_tsv sample_ec.tsv --out_prefix run1 --jobs 4

    --cleanenv matters for reproducibility. Unlike Docker, Apptainer inherits the host
    environment and auto-binds $HOME, /tmp and the current directory, so without it a
    host PYTHONPATH or a stray package in ~/.local can reach into the run and the result
    is no longer the container's.

    MEMORY. The predictor peaks near 5.7 GB on a full metagenome, driven by loading the
    reference layer rather than by the community size. Apptainer does not limit memory
    itself and has no equivalent of `docker run --memory`, so the limit is whatever the
    host or the scheduler imposes: under SLURM ask for `--mem=8G`. Below roughly 8 GB the
    Linux OOM killer takes the process, which surfaces as an unexplained exit 137 rather
    than as a Python error.
